You can now track[latency metrics](https://mastra.ai/reference/observability/tracing/advanced-queries#filter-and-sort-by-time-to-first-chunk-or-token) for every agent and model call in Mastra platform and Studio. Filter and sort traces, spans, and threads by **TTFC** (time to first chunk) and **TTFT** (time to first token) to see where your agents are slow. Or hand off to your coding agent to find and fix issues.


We've spent years optimizing websites with metrics like Time to First Byte and First Contentful Paint, and agents deserve the same attention.[TTFC](https://mastra.ai/reference/observability/tracing/advanced-queries#time-to-first-chunk-ttfc) tells you how long a user waits before seeing anything.[TTFT](https://mastra.ai/reference/observability/tracing/advanced-queries#time-to-first-token-ttft) tells you how long the model takes to start responding. Together, they show you where your agents are slow and give you a meaningful metric to improve.


Your browser does not support the video tag.Mastra platform's Traces view with the time to first chunk and time to first token columns turned on, then Mastra Code querying the same data, tracing the gap to a slow input processor, and suggesting three fixes.


Previously, you could use[durationMs](https://mastra.ai/reference/observability/tracing/advanced-queries#filter-by-root-duration) to see how long a run took, but not how long a user waited for the first response, or whether that wait came from the model or your own code. Mastra now records both metrics on every span, from the model call up to the agent run.


Both TTFC and TTFT are available as fields in[trace queries](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-trace-queries) and[span queries](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-span-queries) , alongside status, environment, and agent name. Latency metrics work on Mastra platform and on self-hosted Mastra projects where` storage.observability` uses DuckDB, PostgreSQL, or ClickHouse. If your trace data lives outside of Mastra, you can bring your existing history over with[trace imports](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-trace-imports-for-mastra-platform) .


## Get started


Latency metrics need a platform access token and your project ID, sent with each request. Use the correct Mastra observability[base URL](https://mastra.ai/docs/mastra-platform/api) for your project's region:


- **US** :` https://observability.mastra.ai`
- **EU** :` https://observability.eu.mastra.ai`


There are four ways to use latency metrics: Mastra CLI, Mastra platform and Studio, Mastra Client SDK, and observability endpoints. The code examples below find runs from the last 7 days where users waited more than 10 seconds for the first chunk, and runs where the model took more than 3 seconds to start responding.


### Mastra CLI


Install or upgrade the Mastra CLI globally:


Terminal


```text
npm   install   -g   mastra@latest
```


note


Requires` mastra@1.30.0` or later, added in[PR #23680](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/23680) .


Use the[mastra api trace query](https://mastra.ai/reference/cli/mastra#mastra-api-trace-query) command to query traces. The CLI reads` MASTRA_PLATFORM_ACCESS_TOKEN` and` MASTRA_PROJECT_ID` from your environment.


Find runs where users waited more than 10 seconds for the first chunk:


Terminal


```text
mastra   api   --url   https://observability.mastra.ai   trace   query   '  {
"timeRange": { "from": "2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z", "to": "2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z" },
"where": { "op": "gt", "left": { "path": "timeToFirstChunkMs" }, "right": { "literal": 10000 } }
}  '
```


Find runs where the model took more than 3 seconds to start responding:


Terminal


```text
mastra   api   --url   https://observability.mastra.ai   trace   query   '  {
"where": { "op": "gt", "left": { "path": "timeToFirstTokenMs" }, "right": { "literal": 3000 } }
}  '
```


### Mastra platform and Studio


note


Studio requires` mastra@1.37.0` and` @mastra/core@1.81.0` or later, added in[PR #28898](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/28898) and[PR #28699](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/28699) .


On Mastra platform and Studio, the **Traces** tab lets you filter and sort by time to first chunk and time to first token. On Mastra platform, turn on the **Time to first chunk** and **Time to first token** columns in the column settings to see the values.


### Mastra Client SDK


Terminal


```text
npm   install   @mastra/client-js@latest
```


note


Requires` @mastra/client-js@1.58.0` or later, added in[PR #28699](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/28699) .


src/client.ts


```text
import   {   MastraClient   }   from   "  @mastra/client-js  "  ;


export   const   mastraClient   =   new   MastraClient  ({
baseUrl:   "  https://observability.mastra.ai  "  ,
headers: {
Authorization:   `  Bearer   ${  process.env.MASTRA_PLATFORM_ACCESS_TOKEN  }`  ,
"  X-Mastra-Project-Id  "  : process.env.MASTRA_PROJECT_ID  !
}
});
```


Find runs where users waited more than 10 seconds for the first chunk:


```text
import   {   mastraClient   }   from   "  ./client  "  ;


const   {   traces   }   =   await   mastraClient.  queryTraces  ({
timeRange: {
from:   "  2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z  "  ,
to:   "  2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z  "
},
where: {
op:   "  gt  "  ,
left: { path:   "  timeToFirstChunkMs  "   },
right: { literal:   10000   }
}
});
```


Find runs where the model took more than 3 seconds to start responding:


```text
import   {   mastraClient   }   from   "  ./client  "  ;


const   {   traces   }   =   await   mastraClient.  queryTraces  ({
timeRange: {
from:   "  2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z  "  ,
to:   "  2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z  "
},
where: {
op:   "  gt  "  ,
left: { path:   "  timeToFirstTokenMs  "   },
right: { literal:   3000   }
}
});
```


### Observability endpoints


You can also send a` POST` request to the trace query endpoint. The request includes` MASTRA_PLATFORM_ACCESS_TOKEN` and` MASTRA_PROJECT_ID` in the headers.


For more information and full configuration options, see:


- [Filter and sort by time to first chunk or token](https://mastra.ai/reference/observability/tracing/advanced-queries#filter-and-sort-by-time-to-first-chunk-or-token)
